  {
    "episode_id": 83600,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83600.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 77,
    "turn_text": "I think people and people don't quite understand, I think, how it works in Washington. It truly is that you are cut out of the process. So your voters in Arizona think, I'm sending Andy Biggs, he's a freedom fighter. He's gonna go and fight. But you're left out of the process, right? Like it really is Pelosi, McConnell, Schumer, McCarthy. It's the big four. They get in a room with lobbyists. They write what the budgets are and they give you 40 minutes to read it before you rubber stamp it. I mean, that really is the way it works, correct?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_22",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "People do not fully understand how politics functions in Washington.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Individual representatives are excluded from meaningful participation in policy formation.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The key decision-making power in Congress resides primarily with four individuals: Nancy Pelosi, Mitch McConnell, Chuck Schumer, and Kevin McCarthy.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Voters in Arizona believe their representative, Andy Biggs, acts as a 'freedom fighter' who will advocate for them.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Lobbyists collaborate with the top four leaders to draft budget proposals.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Members of Congress are given only 40 minutes to review budget documents before being expected to vote on them.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The congressional voting process amounts to a form of rubber-stamping without substantive input.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "True legislative influence requires access to closed-door negotiations and early drafts of legislation.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The public’s perception of representation is based on symbolic identification rather than actual policymaking power.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The credibility of political discourse depends on exposing hidden processes of elite control.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Legislators are expected to support party leadership even when they lack full understanding of proposed bills.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The current system undermines democratic accountability by limiting transparency and deliberation.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The described model reflects a widespread pattern in U.S. legislative behavior, not isolated incidents.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 83600,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83600.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "That's the way it's worse since I've been here, and that's why we advocated for the 12 bills, Benny. And guess what? Okay, I want to add one more thing to this. So, almost two weeks ago now, they ran the Department of Defense funding bill. But they did it with the rule, and four of us voted down the rule. And so it stopped them from being able to just ram and cram because that was the last bill they were going to do. From that point on, they were going to do this continuing resolution where you would keep everything else pretty much like Pelosi, Schumer, Biden had spent with very little policy modification. So we stopped that. The result was they knew that they had to bring bills that had passed out of committee of the appropriations committee to the floor. So we got them to do that. And last week we did something we hadn't done since I've been here, at least to my recollection, is that we actually went to the floor. We ran amendments, argued, debated about those amendments from early morning till two or three in the following morning. So for a 72-hour period, we were able to get four bills brought to the floor for vote. And you know what? I voted for every one of those, even though they weren't the best. Because the process was right. We got some good policy changes. We actually were kind of nipping and tucking around the edge of maybe the budget. You might have a chance to the spending to bring the trajectory down. And you know what? When the moderates voted no on the agriculture bill, which by the way was the best bill of those four bills, nobody said, oh, hey, you guys are a bunch of jerks. You're not team players. What's wrong with you guys? And we're going to take you off your committees. You should be expelled from the Republican Party. They didn't do any of that crap. We just said, oh, well, you know, hey, we had a little problem there with a little hiccup with the agriculture bill. But if I would have voted for that with four or five, eight other people, it would be bigs, needs to be banned for life from eating. That's where it would be, because that's the way they are. And so when you say, yeah, you don't get to participate, that's right. And we finally got to participate. And I, by the way, I have no problem with those people voting the way they think they had to vote for the constituents to take down that bill. That's the process.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_04",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
